    "thm-bairds-counterexample-off-policy-td-diverges": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-markov-decision-process",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, possibly empty, let $\\mathcal R$ be a finite subset of $\\mathbb R$, and for each $s\\in S$ let $A(s)$ be a finite nonempty set. Here **finite** means bijective with a natural number, and the **discrete sigma-algebra** on a set is its power set. Write $D=\\{(s,a):s\\in S,\\ a\\in A(s)\\}$. A **finite Markov decision process (MDP)** consists of these sets and numbers $p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a)\\in[0,1]$, indexed by $(s,a)\\in D$, $s'\\in S$, and $\\rho\\in\\mathcal R$, with\n\n$$ \\sum_{s'\\in S}\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a)=1 \\qquad ((s,a)\\in D). $$\n\nEquip each set used here, including $D$, with its power-set (discrete) sigma-algebra. Thus $p$ is the point-mass form of a probability kernel from $D$ to $S\\times\\mathcal R$: each input assigns a probability measure on $S\\times\\mathcal R$, and every event section is measurable. The joint law allows the next state and reward to be dependent. Define the expected reward and state-transition probabilities by\n\n$$ r(s,a)=\\sum_{s'\\in S}\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}\\rho\\,p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a), \\qquad P(s'\\mid s,a)=\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a). $$\n\nIf $\\mathcal R\\ne\\emptyset$, put $M=\\max_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}|\\rho|$; if $\\mathcal R=\\emptyset$, put $M=0$. A stationary Markov policy is a family of probabilities $\\pi(a\\mid s)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{a\\in A(s)}\\pi(a\\mid s)=1$ for every $s\\in S$. A deterministic policy is a function $\\pi$ with $\\pi(s)\\in A(s)$ for every $s$. When $S=\\emptyset$, these families and policies are the unique empty functions and all statewise formulas are vacuous.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-projected-bellman-error",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a nonempty finite state set of a finite Markov decision process, let $0\\leq\\gamma<1$, let $\\pi$ be a stationary Markov policy, and let $\\mu:S\\to[0,1]$ satisfy $\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)=1$. The function $\\mu$ is a fixed state-weighting distribution; it need not be stationary for $\\pi$. Let $d$ be a positive integer, let $\\phi:S\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a feature map, and for $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$ put $v_w(s)=\\phi(s)^\\mathsf T w$.\n\nFor real functions $f,g:S\\to\\mathbb R$, write $f\\sim_\\mu g$ when $\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)(f(s)-g(s))^2=0$. Let $[f]_\\mu$ denote the equivalence class of $f$ and define $\\langle[f]_{\\mu},[g]_{\\mu}\\rangle_\\mu=\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)f(s)g(s)$ and $\\|[f]_\\mu\\|_\\mu^2=\\langle[f]_{\\mu},[f]_{\\mu}\\rangle_\\mu$. Let $H_\\mu$ be the resulting finite-dimensional real inner-product space, and let $V_\\mu$ be the span in $H_\\mu$ of the equivalence classes of the $d$ coordinate feature functions.\n\nFor $h\\in H_\\mu$, let $\\Pi_\\mu h$ be the unique vector in $V_\\mu$ whose difference from $h$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V_\\mu$. The Bellman-error vector for $v_w$ is $\\delta_w=[T^\\pi v_w-v_w]_\\mu$. Define the mean square Bellman error and mean square projected Bellman error by $\\operatorname{BE}_\\mu(w)=\\|\\delta_w\\|_\\mu^2$ and $\\operatorname{PBE}_\\mu(w)=\\|\\Pi_\\mu\\delta_w\\|_\\mu^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-policy-bellman-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite MDP, a discount $0\\le\\gamma<1$, and a stationary Markov policy $\\pi$, define the **policy Bellman operator** $T^\\pi:\\mathbb R^S\\to\\mathbb R^S$ by\n\n$$ (T^\\pi V)(s)=\\sum_{a\\in A(s)}\\pi(a\\mid s)\\left[r(s,a)+\\gamma\\sum_{s'\\in S}P(s'\\mid s,a)V(s')\\right]\\qquad(s\\in S). $$\n\nIf $S=\\emptyset$, this is the unique map from $\\mathbb R^S$ to itself.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a vector space over a field $F$ and let $T:V\\to V$ be linear. A scalar $\\lambda\\in F$ is an **eigenvalue** of $T$ when there is a nonzero vector $v\\in V$ such that\n\n$$T(v)=\\lambda v.$$\n\nSuch a vector is an **eigenvector belonging to $\\lambda$**. The **eigenspace** belonging to $\\lambda$ is\n\n$$E_\\lambda(T):=\\ker(T-\\lambda I_V)=\\{v\\in V:T(v)=\\lambda v\\}.$$\n\nThus $E_\\lambda(T)$ is a linear subspace and always contains $0_V$, while $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue exactly when $E_\\lambda(T)\\ne\\{0_V\\}$. The **spectrum of $T$ over $F$** is\n\n$$\\sigma_F(T):=\\{\\lambda\\in F:\\lambda\\text{ is an eigenvalue of }T\\}.$$\n\nIn particular, the unique endomorphism of the zero space has empty spectrum.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and let $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$, with the Euclidean\ninner product and the Euclidean norm as in [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Then:\n\n1. **Cauchy-Schwarz.**\n   $$\\lvert\\langle x,y\\rangle\\rvert \\;\\le\\; \\lVert x\\rVert_2\\,\\lVert y\\rVert_2 ,$$\n   with equality if and only if there is a pair $(\\lambda,\\mu) \\ne (0,0)$ of\n   reals with $\\lambda x_k = \\mu y_k$ for every $k < n$.\n2. **$\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$**\n   ([[def-norm-and-normed-space]]), for **every** $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$; the metric\n   it induces is $d_2$ of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] whenever $n \\ge 1$.\n3. **Parallelogram law.**\n   $$\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} + \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2} \\;=\\; 2\\lVert x\\rVert_2^{2} + 2\\lVert y\\rVert_2^{2} .$$\n4. **Polarisation.**\n   $$\\langle x,y\\rangle \\;=\\; \\tfrac14\\Bigl(\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} - \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2}\\Bigr),$$\n   so the inner product is recovered from the norm it induces.\n\n**Clause 1 is a citation, not a new proof.** The inequality and its equality case\nare the published [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite]], stated there for two lists of\nreals; all that happens below is that it is read in the vector notation of\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Re-proving it here would put two proofs of one\nstatement in the library.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-geometric-sequence-null",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and let $r^k$ be the integer power ([[def-integer-power]]).\n\n1. If $|r| < 1$ then $(r^k)$ is **null**, that is $r^k \\to 0$\n   ([[def-real-limit]]).\n2. If $|r| > 1$ then $(|r|^k)$ **diverges to $+\\infty$**\n   ([[def-divergence-to-infinity]]).\n\nClaim 2 is stated for $|r|^k$ and not for $r^k$ on purpose: for $r < -1$ the\nterms $r^k$ alternate in sign and are unbounded, so they neither converge nor\ndiverge to $+\\infty$; what is true of them is the statement about their absolute\nvalues.\n\nBoth claims come from Bernoulli's inequality ([[lem-bernoulli-inequality]]) and\nthe Archimedean property. Nothing here needs the least-upper-bound property\nexcept through [[thm-of-archimedean]] and [[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]].",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "thm-bairds-counterexample-off-policy-td-diverges-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $\\phi_i=2e_i+e_8$ for $1\\leq i\\leq6$ and $\\phi_7=e_7+2e_8$. The importance ratio is $7$ on solid and $0$ on dashed. Under behavior, every next-state probability is $1/7$: each upper state is reached with probability $(6/7)(1/6)$ and $s_7$ with probability $1/7$. Thus the expected TD update direction is $\\frac17\\sum_{i=1}^7\\phi_i(\\gamma\\phi_7^\\mathsf T w-\\phi_i^\\mathsf T w)$. The target Bellman value is $\\gamma\\phi_7^\\mathsf T w$ at every state, so this also gives the synchronous expected DP direction. Both recurrences are $w^+=(I+\\alpha M)w$, where $M=\\frac17\\sum_i\\phi_i(\\gamma\\phi_7-\\phi_i)^\\mathsf T$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bairds-counterexample-off-policy-td-diverges-2-1",
          "claim": "The update preserves equality of the first six coordinates. Write their common value as $a$, and put $b=w_7$, $c=w_8$. Set $q_u=\\gamma(b+2c)-(2a+c)=-2a+\\gamma b+(2\\gamma-1)c$ and $q_7=(\\gamma-1)(b+2c)$. By step 1.1, each upper feature contributes $2q_u$ to its own coordinate and $q_u$ to coordinate $8$, while $\\phi_7$ contributes $q_7$ to coordinate $7$ and $2q_7$ to coordinate $8$, all with factor $\\alpha/7$. Thus $a^+=a+2\\alpha q_u/7$, $b^+=b+\\alpha q_7/7$, and $c^+=c+\\alpha(6q_u+2q_7)/7$. Hence $y=(a,b,c)^\\mathsf T$ follows $y^+=(I+\\alpha B/7)y$, where $B=\\begin{pmatrix}-4&2\\gamma&4\\gamma-2\\\\0&\\gamma-1&2\\gamma-2\\\\-12&8\\gamma-2&16\\gamma-10\\end{pmatrix}$ and $y_0=(1,10,1)^\\mathsf T$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bairds-counterexample-off-policy-td-diverges-3-1",
          "claim": "At $\\gamma=99/100$, let $q(t)=t^2-(183/100)t+13/50$. Its distinct roots are $t_\\pm=(183\\pm\\sqrt{23089})/200$, both positive because $23089<183^2$ and their sum and product are positive. Put $t=t_+$, $u=-12/(t+4)$, $v=(2\\gamma u+8\\gamma-2)/(t+1-\\gamma)$, and $\\ell=(u,v,1)$. By the definitions of $u$ and $v$, the first two coordinates of $\\ell B=t\\ell$ hold.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bairds-counterexample-off-policy-td-diverges-4-1",
          "claim": "For the third coordinate, substitution gives $(4\\gamma-2)u+(2\\gamma-2)v+(16\\gamma-10)-t=\\frac{-5000tq(t)}{50(t+4)(100t+1)}=0$; the denominator is nonzero since $t>0$, and $q(t)=0$. Thus $\\ell B=t\\ell$, so $(B/7)^\\mathsf T\\ell^\\mathsf T=(t/7)\\ell^\\mathsf T$. Since $\\ell=(u,v,1)\\ne0$, [F3] makes $t/7>0$ an eigenvalue of the transpose map.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bairds-counterexample-off-policy-td-diverges-4-2",
          "claim": "The quadratic in step 3.1 has $q(8/5)=-27/250<0$ and $q(9/5)=103/500>0$. Since it has positive leading coefficient and two distinct roots, the first sign places $8/5$ between the roots, and the second places $9/5$ to the right of $t_+$; hence $8/5<t<9/5$. Therefore $u=-12/(t+4)>-15/7$. Using $\\gamma=99/100$ and $u>-15/7$ gives $2\\gamma u+8\\gamma-2>2(99/100)(-15/7)+8(99/100)-2=587/350$. Also $0<t+1-\\gamma<181/100$, so $v>(587/350)/(181/100)=58700/63350>9/10$. It follows that $\\ell y_0=u+10v+1>-15/7+10=55/7>0$.",
          "step": "4.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bairds-counterexample-off-policy-td-diverges-5-1",
          "claim": "For every $\\alpha>0$, $r=1+\\alpha t/7>1$, so [F5] gives $r^n\\to+\\infty$. Induction using the recurrence in step 2.1 and the eigenrelation in step 4.1 yields $\\ell y_n=r^n\\ell y_0\\to+\\infty$, since step 4.2 proves $\\ell y_0>0$. As $\\ell\\neq0$, [F4] implies $\\|y_n\\|_2\\geq|\\ell y_n|/\\|\\ell\\|_2\\to\\infty$. The first six full weights equal $a_n$, so $\\|w_n\\|_2^2=6a_n^2+b_n^2+c_n^2\\geq\\|y_n\\|_2^2$. Step 1.1 showed that the expected TD and synchronous DP recurrences coincide, proving both divergences.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
