    "thm-bellman-error-unlearnability-example": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-markov-decision-process",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, possibly empty, let $\\mathcal R$ be a finite subset of $\\mathbb R$, and for each $s\\in S$ let $A(s)$ be a finite nonempty set. Here **finite** means bijective with a natural number, and the **discrete sigma-algebra** on a set is its power set. Write $D=\\{(s,a):s\\in S,\\ a\\in A(s)\\}$. A **finite Markov decision process (MDP)** consists of these sets and numbers $p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a)\\in[0,1]$, indexed by $(s,a)\\in D$, $s'\\in S$, and $\\rho\\in\\mathcal R$, with\n\n$$ \\sum_{s'\\in S}\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a)=1 \\qquad ((s,a)\\in D). $$\n\nEquip each set used here, including $D$, with its power-set (discrete) sigma-algebra. Thus $p$ is the point-mass form of a probability kernel from $D$ to $S\\times\\mathcal R$: each input assigns a probability measure on $S\\times\\mathcal R$, and every event section is measurable. The joint law allows the next state and reward to be dependent. Define the expected reward and state-transition probabilities by\n\n$$ r(s,a)=\\sum_{s'\\in S}\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}\\rho\\,p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a), \\qquad P(s'\\mid s,a)=\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a). $$\n\nIf $\\mathcal R\\ne\\emptyset$, put $M=\\max_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}|\\rho|$; if $\\mathcal R=\\emptyset$, put $M=0$. A stationary Markov policy is a family of probabilities $\\pi(a\\mid s)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{a\\in A(s)}\\pi(a\\mid s)=1$ for every $s\\in S$. A deterministic policy is a function $\\pi$ with $\\pi(s)\\in A(s)$ for every $s$. When $S=\\emptyset$, these families and policies are the unique empty functions and all statewise formulas are vacuous.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-projected-bellman-error",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a nonempty finite state set of a finite Markov decision process, let $0\\leq\\gamma<1$, let $\\pi$ be a stationary Markov policy, and let $\\mu:S\\to[0,1]$ satisfy $\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)=1$. The function $\\mu$ is a fixed state-weighting distribution; it need not be stationary for $\\pi$. Let $d$ be a positive integer, let $\\phi:S\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a feature map, and for $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$ put $v_w(s)=\\phi(s)^\\mathsf T w$.\n\nFor real functions $f,g:S\\to\\mathbb R$, write $f\\sim_\\mu g$ when $\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)(f(s)-g(s))^2=0$. Let $[f]_\\mu$ denote the equivalence class of $f$ and define $\\langle[f]_{\\mu},[g]_{\\mu}\\rangle_\\mu=\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)f(s)g(s)$ and $\\|[f]_\\mu\\|_\\mu^2=\\langle[f]_{\\mu},[f]_{\\mu}\\rangle_\\mu$. Let $H_\\mu$ be the resulting finite-dimensional real inner-product space, and let $V_\\mu$ be the span in $H_\\mu$ of the equivalence classes of the $d$ coordinate feature functions.\n\nFor $h\\in H_\\mu$, let $\\Pi_\\mu h$ be the unique vector in $V_\\mu$ whose difference from $h$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V_\\mu$. The Bellman-error vector for $v_w$ is $\\delta_w=[T^\\pi v_w-v_w]_\\mu$. Define the mean square Bellman error and mean square projected Bellman error by $\\operatorname{BE}_\\mu(w)=\\|\\delta_w\\|_\\mu^2$ and $\\operatorname{PBE}_\\mu(w)=\\|\\Pi_\\mu\\delta_w\\|_\\mu^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-policy-bellman-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite MDP, a discount $0\\le\\gamma<1$, and a stationary Markov policy $\\pi$, define the **policy Bellman operator** $T^\\pi:\\mathbb R^S\\to\\mathbb R^S$ by\n\n$$ (T^\\pi V)(s)=\\sum_{a\\in A(s)}\\pi(a\\mid s)\\left[r(s,a)+\\gamma\\sum_{s'\\in S}P(s'\\mid s,a)V(s')\\right]\\qquad(s\\in S). $$\n\nIf $S=\\emptyset$, this is the unique map from $\\mathbb R^S$ to itself.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-finite-mdp-policy-induces-reward-state-chain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let a finite MDP have nonempty state set $S$, and let $\\pi$ be a stationary Markov policy. Put $E=S\\times(\\mathcal R\\cup\\{0\\})$ with its discrete sigma-algebra and define, for $x=(s,\\eta)\\in E$ and $B\\subseteq E$,\n\n$$ K_\\pi(x,B)=\\sum_{a\\in A(s)}\\pi(a\\mid s) \\sum_{\\substack{s'\\in S\\\\\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}}1_B(s',\\rho)\\,p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a). $$\n\nThen $K_\\pi$ is a probability kernel on $E$. For each initial state $s\\in S$, there is a probability law on $E^{\\mathbb N}$ whose coordinate process starts at $(s,0)$ and is a time-homogeneous Markov chain with transition kernel $K_\\pi$ relative to its natural filtration.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-ionescu-tulcea-construction-of-a-markov-chain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Choice. Let $(E_n,\\mathcal E_n)_{n\\ge0}$ be measurable spaces, let\n$\\mu_0$ be a probability measure on $E_0$, and, for each $n\\ge0$, let $K_n$ be\na probability kernel from $E_0\\times\\cdots\\times E_n$ to $E_{n+1}$. There is a\nunique probability measure $P$ on\n$$ \\left(\\prod_{n\\ge0}E_n,\\ \\bigotimes_{n\\ge0}\\mathcal E_n\\right) $$ whose finite-prefix laws are the prescribed iterated integrals $$ \\mu_0(dx_0)K_0(x_0,dx_1)K_1(x_0,x_1,dx_2)\\cdots K_{r-1}(x_0,\\ldots,x_{r-1},dx_r). $$\nIn particular, if every $E_n=E$ and\n$K_n(x_0,\\ldots,x_n,A)=K(x_n,A)$, the coordinate process is a homogeneous\n$K$-chain with initial law $\\mu_0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "thm-bellman-error-unlearnability-example-1-1",
          "claim": "The transition matrices, with states ordered as displayed in the Statement, are $P_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1/2&1/2\\end{pmatrix}$ and $P_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1/2&1/2\\\\1&0&0\\\\0&1/2&1/2\\end{pmatrix}$. Every row sums to one. Direct multiplication gives $\\mu_1P_1=(1/3,2/3)=\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2P_2=(1/3,1/3,1/3)=\\mu_2$. The observed feature has mass $1/3$ at $A$ and $2/3$ at feature $B$ in each process.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bellman-error-unlearnability-example-1-2",
          "claim": "Represent each MRP as a one-action finite MDP. Under Choice, [F3] supplies a state/reward path law for each fixed initial state; the finite mixture of these laws with weights $\\mu_1$ or $\\mu_2$ gives the stated random initial state. Let $Y_n=(\\psi(S_n),R_{n+1})$ and set $x_A=((1,0),0)$, $x_+=((0,1),1)$, and $x_-=((0,1),-1)$. In MRP 1, the initial state law and its outgoing rewards give probability $1/3$ to each of $x_A,x_+,x_-$; MRP 2 gives the same probabilities. In each process the transition kernel of $Y_n$ is $x_A\\mapsto\\frac12x_++\\frac12x_-$, $x_+\\mapsto x_A$, and $x_-\\mapsto\\frac12x_++\\frac12x_-$. Indeed, in MRP 1, $x_A$ comes from state $A$ and the next state is $B$, whose outgoing reward is equally likely $1$ or $-1$; $x_+$ records the transition $B\\to A$, so the next observation is $x_A$; and $x_-$ records $B\\to B$, after which the next outgoing reward is equally likely $1$ or $-1$. In MRP 2, $x_A$ comes from state $A$, whose next state is $B$ or $B'$ equally; $x_+$ identifies state $B$, whose next state is $A$; and $x_-$ identifies state $B'$, whose next state is $B$ or $B'$ equally. Thus both observed processes have the same initial law $\\nu$ and transition kernel $K$, and their finite-prefix laws factor as $\\nu(x_0)\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}K(x_k,\\{x_{k+1}\\})$ by repeated conditioning. By [F4], the path law with these finite-prefix laws is unique, so their observable path laws agree.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "induction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bellman-error-unlearnability-example-2-1",
          "claim": "In MRP 1 the conditional expected Bellman residual at $A$ is $\\gamma b-a$, because $A$ moves to $B$ with reward zero. At $B$, expected reward is $(1-1)/2=0$ and the expected next value is $(a+b)/2$, so the residual is $\\gamma(a+b)/2-b$. Weighting their squares by $\\mu_1$ gives the stated $\\operatorname{BE}_1$. In MRP 2, the residuals at $A,B,B'$ are respectively $\\gamma b-a$, $1+\\gamma a-b$, and $-1+\\gamma b-b=-1+(\\gamma-1)b$, which gives the stated $\\operatorname{BE}_2$ under $\\mu_2$. At $(0,0)$ the first objective is zero and the second is $2/3$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bellman-error-unlearnability-example-3-1",
          "claim": "Since $\\operatorname{BE}_1$ is a sum of nonnegative squares and vanishes at $(0,0)$, that point is a minimizer. If it vanishes at $(a,b)$, its first residual gives $a=\\gamma b$ and its second gives $b=\\gamma(a+b)/2=\\gamma(\\gamma+1)b/2$. Since $\\gamma(\\gamma+1)/2<1$ for $0\\leq\\gamma<1$, $b=0$ and then $a=0$, proving uniqueness.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bellman-error-unlearnability-example-3-2",
          "claim": "Let $r_A=\\gamma b-a$, $r_B=1+\\gamma a-b$, and $r_{B'}=-1+(\\gamma-1)b$. Then $3\\operatorname{BE}_2=r_A^2+r_B^2+r_{B'}^2$. Its residual matrix is $X=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&\\gamma\\\\\\gamma&-1\\\\0&\\gamma-1\\end{pmatrix}$, with residual vector $X(a,b)^\\mathsf T+(0,1,-1)^\\mathsf T$. At $(a,b)=(-\\gamma/D,\\gamma/(2D))$, direct substitution gives $r_B=(\\gamma+2)/(2D)$, $r_A=\\gamma r_B$, and $r_{B'}=-(\\gamma+1)r_B$. Hence $X^\\mathsf T(r_A,r_B,r_{B'})^\\mathsf T=0$. For any increment $h\\in\\mathbb R^2$, expanding the squared residual gives $3(\\operatorname{BE}_2(w+h)-\\operatorname{BE}_2(w))=\\|Xh\\|_2^2$ at this point. If $Xh=0$, its first two rows give $h_1=\\gamma h_2$ and $\\gamma h_1=h_2$, so $(1-\\gamma^2)h_2=0$; because $\\gamma<1$, $h=0$. Thus the objective is strictly larger at every nonzero perturbation and the stated point is the unique global minimizer. Its minimum value is $\\frac13\\big(\\gamma^2+1+(\\gamma+1)^2\\big)\\big(\\frac{\\gamma+2}{2D}\\big)^2=(\\gamma+2)^2/(6D)$.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-bellman-error-unlearnability-example-4-1",
          "claim": "The observable path laws agree by step 1.2, but the Bellman-error functions differ because their values at $(0,0)$ are $0$ and $2/3$. For $0<\\gamma<1$, their unique minimizers also differ because MRP 1 has minimizer $(0,0)$ whereas MRP 2 has first coordinate $-\\gamma/D<0$. Any quantity determined only by the observable law must be the same for two models with that law; it cannot equal these distinct objectives or, for positive discount, both minimizing parameters. This proves the non-identifiability claims.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 3.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
