        {"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The Statement contains no biconditional.","template_review":{"upheld":true,"by":"owner-delegated:/root/calibration_surrogate_repair","reason":"The two explicitly specified reward processes have identical observable sequence laws but different Bellman-error objectives and, at positive discount, different minimizing parameters. This is a witness of nonidentification, not an iff claim about observability.","item_sha256":"c878ae883149378ce4f68a009758ade6dc6f4b7cb6b1fc9120c9f13a6ece4063","row_sha256":"b5418604072e4d20720cfe4a39cf0838dc85680201091986c51f484580ce84f6"},"template_review_history":[{"upheld":true,"by":"/root/ucrl2_repair","reason":"Finite transition calculations identify a common observed Markov kernel for the two different hidden-state models. Choice-qualified path-law uniqueness extends that agreement to infinite observations, while direct quadratic residual calculations give unequal objectives and distinct interior-discount minimizers without a converse characterization.","item_sha256":"ab954f28d2321389781009ebf12744e2eb21ef1c47274ae7d252ced43d00111f","row_sha256":"b5418604072e4d20720cfe4a39cf0838dc85680201091986c51f484580ce84f6"}]},
        {"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The Statement contains no biconditional.","template_review":{"upheld":true,"by":"owner-delegated:/root/calibration_surrogate_repair","reason":"The two explicitly specified reward processes have identical observable sequence laws but different Bellman-error objectives and, at positive discount, different minimizing parameters. This is a witness of nonidentification, not an iff claim about observability.","item_sha256":"c878ae883149378ce4f68a009758ade6dc6f4b7cb6b1fc9120c9f13a6ece4063","row_sha256":"cb3270b358350459a2265f8060a8b95b1014291534dda82ce7b6e01df30ae0cd"},"template_review_history":[{"upheld":true,"by":"/root/ucrl2_repair","reason":"Finite transition calculations identify a common observed Markov kernel for the two different hidden-state models. Choice-qualified path-law uniqueness extends that agreement to infinite observations, while direct quadratic residual calculations give unequal objectives and distinct interior-discount minimizers without a converse characterization.","item_sha256":"ab954f28d2321389781009ebf12744e2eb21ef1c47274ae7d252ced43d00111f","row_sha256":"cb3270b358350459a2265f8060a8b95b1014291534dda82ce7b6e01df30ae0cd"}]}
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-85",
        "notes": "Read all six proof steps, complete Statement, endpoint remark and two MRP diagrams, and actual finite-MDP/path-law/Bellman suppliers. Both normalized joint laws have stated stationary vectors. For Y=(current feature,outgoing reward), each observed symbol fixes the next hidden-state distribution independently of past; the common three-symbol initial law and kernel give equal cylinder laws and path laws under explicit AC. Conditional expected residuals, residual matrix, normal equations and injectivity of X for gamma<1 prove unique minima. At gamma=0 both minimizers are zero while objectives differ; title repair correctly limits minimizer distinction to positive discount. gamma=1 is excluded and remark correctly distinguishes limit from endpoint. Every diagram arrow/reward/probability matches its stated kernel. Published F2 nonemptiness citation defect is nonfatal and does not invalidate the path-law argument; owner maintenance is reported separately."
      }
    },
    "ex-on-policy-one-state-linear-td-is-stable": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-markov-decision-process",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, possibly empty, let $\\mathcal R$ be a finite subset of $\\mathbb R$, and for each $s\\in S$ let $A(s)$ be a finite nonempty set. Here **finite** means bijective with a natural number, and the **discrete sigma-algebra** on a set is its power set. Write $D=\\{(s,a):s\\in S,\\ a\\in A(s)\\}$. A **finite Markov decision process (MDP)** consists of these sets and numbers $p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a)\\in[0,1]$, indexed by $(s,a)\\in D$, $s'\\in S$, and $\\rho\\in\\mathcal R$, with\n\n$$ \\sum_{s'\\in S}\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a)=1 \\qquad ((s,a)\\in D). $$\n\nEquip each set used here, including $D$, with its power-set (discrete) sigma-algebra. Thus $p$ is the point-mass form of a probability kernel from $D$ to $S\\times\\mathcal R$: each input assigns a probability measure on $S\\times\\mathcal R$, and every event section is measurable. The joint law allows the next state and reward to be dependent. Define the expected reward and state-transition probabilities by\n\n$$ r(s,a)=\\sum_{s'\\in S}\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}\\rho\\,p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a), \\qquad P(s'\\mid s,a)=\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a). $$\n\nIf $\\mathcal R\\ne\\emptyset$, put $M=\\max_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}|\\rho|$; if $\\mathcal R=\\emptyset$, put $M=0$. A stationary Markov policy is a family of probabilities $\\pi(a\\mid s)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{a\\in A(s)}\\pi(a\\mid s)=1$ for every $s\\in S$. A deterministic policy is a function $\\pi$ with $\\pi(s)\\in A(s)$ for every $s$. When $S=\\emptyset$, these families and policies are the unique empty functions and all statewise formulas are vacuous.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-projected-bellman-error",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a nonempty finite state set of a finite Markov decision process, let $0\\leq\\gamma<1$, let $\\pi$ be a stationary Markov policy, and let $\\mu:S\\to[0,1]$ satisfy $\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)=1$. The function $\\mu$ is a fixed state-weighting distribution; it need not be stationary for $\\pi$. Let $d$ be a positive integer, let $\\phi:S\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a feature map, and for $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$ put $v_w(s)=\\phi(s)^\\mathsf T w$.\n\nFor real functions $f,g:S\\to\\mathbb R$, write $f\\sim_\\mu g$ when $\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)(f(s)-g(s))^2=0$. Let $[f]_\\mu$ denote the equivalence class of $f$ and define $\\langle[f]_{\\mu},[g]_{\\mu}\\rangle_\\mu=\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)f(s)g(s)$ and $\\|[f]_\\mu\\|_\\mu^2=\\langle[f]_{\\mu},[f]_{\\mu}\\rangle_\\mu$. Let $H_\\mu$ be the resulting finite-dimensional real inner-product space, and let $V_\\mu$ be the span in $H_\\mu$ of the equivalence classes of the $d$ coordinate feature functions.\n\nFor $h\\in H_\\mu$, let $\\Pi_\\mu h$ be the unique vector in $V_\\mu$ whose difference from $h$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V_\\mu$. The Bellman-error vector for $v_w$ is $\\delta_w=[T^\\pi v_w-v_w]_\\mu$. Define the mean square Bellman error and mean square projected Bellman error by $\\operatorname{BE}_\\mu(w)=\\|\\delta_w\\|_\\mu^2$ and $\\operatorname{PBE}_\\mu(w)=\\|\\Pi_\\mu\\delta_w\\|_\\mu^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-geometric-sequence-null",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and let $r^k$ be the integer power ([[def-integer-power]]).\n\n1. If $|r| < 1$ then $(r^k)$ is **null**, that is $r^k \\to 0$\n   ([[def-real-limit]]).\n2. If $|r| > 1$ then $(|r|^k)$ **diverges to $+\\infty$**\n   ([[def-divergence-to-infinity]]).\n\nClaim 2 is stated for $|r|^k$ and not for $r^k$ on purpose: for $r < -1$ the\nterms $r^k$ alternate in sign and are unbounded, so they neither converge nor\ndiverge to $+\\infty$; what is true of them is the statement about their absolute\nvalues.\n\nBoth claims come from Bernoulli's inequality ([[lem-bernoulli-inequality]]) and\nthe Archimedean property. Nothing here needs the least-upper-bound property\nexcept through [[thm-of-archimedean]] and [[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "ex-on-policy-one-state-linear-td-is-stable-1-1",
          "claim": "The behavior and target policies are the same unique action, so the importance ratio is $1$. The next state is always $s$, hence the TD error at weight $w_n$ is $0+\\gamma w_n-w_n=-(1-\\gamma)w_n$. Substitution in the update gives $w_{n+1}=q w_n$, where $q=1-\\alpha(1-\\gamma)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-on-policy-one-state-linear-td-is-stable-2-1",
          "claim": "Because $0<\\alpha\\leq1$ and $0\\leq\\gamma<1$, one has $\\gamma\\leq q<1$, so $|q|<1$. Induction gives $w_n=q^n w_0$ for every $n$, and [F3] implies $w_n\\to0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "induction",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
