    "ex-matched-observation-mrps-have-distinct-bellman-errors": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-projected-bellman-error",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a nonempty finite state set of a finite Markov decision process, let $0\\leq\\gamma<1$, let $\\pi$ be a stationary Markov policy, and let $\\mu:S\\to[0,1]$ satisfy $\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)=1$. The function $\\mu$ is a fixed state-weighting distribution; it need not be stationary for $\\pi$. Let $d$ be a positive integer, let $\\phi:S\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a feature map, and for $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$ put $v_w(s)=\\phi(s)^\\mathsf T w$.\n\nFor real functions $f,g:S\\to\\mathbb R$, write $f\\sim_\\mu g$ when $\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)(f(s)-g(s))^2=0$. Let $[f]_\\mu$ denote the equivalence class of $f$ and define $\\langle[f]_{\\mu},[g]_{\\mu}\\rangle_\\mu=\\sum_{s\\in S}\\mu(s)f(s)g(s)$ and $\\|[f]_\\mu\\|_\\mu^2=\\langle[f]_{\\mu},[f]_{\\mu}\\rangle_\\mu$. Let $H_\\mu$ be the resulting finite-dimensional real inner-product space, and let $V_\\mu$ be the span in $H_\\mu$ of the equivalence classes of the $d$ coordinate feature functions.\n\nFor $h\\in H_\\mu$, let $\\Pi_\\mu h$ be the unique vector in $V_\\mu$ whose difference from $h$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V_\\mu$. The Bellman-error vector for $v_w$ is $\\delta_w=[T^\\pi v_w-v_w]_\\mu$. Define the mean square Bellman error and mean square projected Bellman error by $\\operatorname{BE}_\\mu(w)=\\|\\delta_w\\|_\\mu^2$ and $\\operatorname{PBE}_\\mu(w)=\\|\\Pi_\\mu\\delta_w\\|_\\mu^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-bellman-error-unlearnability-example",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice to form the infinite trajectory laws. Fix $0\\leq\\gamma<1$. An actionless finite Markov reward process (MRP) here means a nonempty finite state set with a joint probability law for next state and reward at each state. Consider the following two MRPs, each with its stated stationary state-weighting distribution, meaning that one transition leaves the distribution unchanged.\n\nIn MRP 1 the state set is $\\{A,B\\}$, with transitions $A\\to B$ receiving reward $0$ deterministically and $B\\to A$ receiving reward $1$ with probability $1/2$ or $B\\to B$ receiving reward $-1$ with probability $1/2$. Its stationary weighting is $\\mu_1(A)=1/3$, $\\mu_1(B)=2/3$.\n\nIn MRP 2 the state set is $\\{A,B,B'\\}$, with transitions $A\\to B$ and $A\\to B'$ each receiving reward $0$ with probability $1/2$, $B\\to A$ receiving reward $1$ deterministically, and $B'\\to B$ or $B'\\to B'$ each receiving reward $-1$ with probability $1/2$. Its stationary weighting is $\\mu_2(A)=\\mu_2(B)=\\mu_2(B')=1/3$.\n\nIn both processes the observed feature is $\\psi(A)=(1,0)$ and $\\psi(B)=\\psi(B')=(0,1)$, so a parameter $w=(a,b)^\\mathsf T$ assigns values $v_w(A)=a$ and $v_w(B)=v_w(B')=b$. Starting from the stated stationary weighting, the two processes induce the same probability law on the observed feature/reward sequence $((\\psi(S_n),R_{n+1}))_{n\\geq0}$. Their mean square Bellman errors, defined as the stationary-weighted square of the conditional expected one-step TD error, are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{BE}_1(a,b)&=\\frac13(\\gamma b-a)^2+\\frac23\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}(a+b)-b\\right)^2,\\\\ \\operatorname{BE}_2(a,b)&=\\frac13\\left((\\gamma b-a)^2+(1+\\gamma a-b)^2+(-1+(\\gamma-1)b)^2\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMRP 1 has the unique minimizer $(a,b)=(0,0)$ and minimum $0$. With $D=1+\\gamma+\\gamma^2$, MRP 2 has the unique minimizer $(a,b)=\\left(-\\frac{\\gamma}{D},\\frac{\\gamma}{2D}\\right)$ and minimum $\\frac{(\\gamma+2)^2}{6D}$; also $\\operatorname{BE}_2(0,0)=2/3$. For every $0<\\gamma<1$ the minimizing parameters differ. Thus the Bellman-error objective and, for positive discount, its minimizing parameter are not determined by the observable feature/reward sequence law. An MRP here has no action-dependent policy; it is not an unseen-action offline-MDP policy-impossibility claim.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "ex-matched-observation-mrps-have-distinct-bellman-errors-1-1",
          "claim": "At $\\gamma=1/2$, $D=1+\\gamma+\\gamma^2=7/4$. Substitution in [F2] gives MRP 2\u2019s minimizing parameter $(-2/7,1/7)$ and minimum $(5/2)^2/(6\\cdot7/4)=25/42$. The same substitution gives MRP 1\u2019s minimizing parameter $(0,0)$ and minimum $0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-matched-observation-mrps-have-distinct-bellman-errors-2-1",
          "claim": "At $w=(0,0)$ the MRP 2 residuals are $0,1,-1$, so [F1] gives $\\operatorname{BE}_2(0,0)=(0^2+1^2+(-1)^2)/3=2/3$. The MRP pair and common observation law are the $\\gamma=1/2$ instance of [F2], whose path-law claim uses [A1], completing the example.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
